      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>The Architecture of Allowances Under the 8th Central Pay Commission</h2>
        <p>
          In the remuneration architecture of Indian Central and State Government employees, basic pay forms only the foundational tier. A significant percentage of an employee's monthly disposable income is determined by two vital compensatory mechanisms: <strong>Dearness Allowance (DA)</strong> and <strong>House Rent Allowance (HRA)</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          These two allowances serve distinct economic mandates:
        </p>
        <ul class="bullet-feature-list">
          <li><strong>Dearness Allowance (DA):</strong> A variable, macro-inflation compensatory allowance designed to protect real earnings from erosion caused by consumer commodity inflation.</li>
          <li><strong>House Rent Allowance (HRA):</strong> A geographic allowance meant to bridge the disparity in residential rental costs across India's metropolitan, semi-urban, and rural territories when government residential quarters (General Pool Residential Accommodation - GPRA) are unavailable.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Whenever a new Central Pay Commission takes effect, the computational baselines for both DA and HRA undergo comprehensive restructuring. Calculating these values accurately requires a thorough understanding of retail price re-basing, statutory minimum floor protections, and the transition of city categorization tiers.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Dearness Allowance Merger & The 0% Reset Mechanism</h2>
        <p>
          The most frequently misunderstood feature of any Pay Commission transition is the apparent "reduction" of Dearness Allowance back to zero. Central employees accustomed to collecting a DA rate surpassing 50% often inquire why their pay slips suddenly display <strong>0% DA</strong> following the gazette implementation.
        </p>
        <p>
          The process relies on structured macroeconomic accounting:
        </p>
        <div class="callout-box">
          <h4>The Compounding Mechanics of DA Absorption:</h4>
          <p>
            Dearness Allowance does not vanish; it is <strong>subsumed permanently into the basic pay</strong> through the fitment factor multiplier. 
          </p>
          <p>
            When the fitment factor (e.g., 2.28x) is applied, the 50%+ accumulated DA is merged into the base. Because the base pay itself has more than doubled, retaining the old 50% DA would amount to double-counting inflation. Thus, the index is formally reset to <strong>0%</strong>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The Compounding Advantage:</strong> When the Ministry of Finance announces the first post-revision DA hike of 3% or 4%, it is calculated on the <em>new, higher basic pay</em>. A 3% DA increase on an 8th CPC basic pay of ₹80,712 yields <strong>₹2,421 per month</strong>, compared to 3% on the old 7th CPC basic of ₹35,400 which yielded merely <strong>₹1,062 per month</strong>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- DATA MATRIX TABLE -->
      <section class="editorial-chapter">
