                <td>All small towns, tehsil headquarters, rural border outposts, non-classified belts.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          Under the 7th CPC guidelines, HRA percentages were dynamically linked to DA milestones: when DA surpassed 25%, HRA transitioned to 27%, 18%, and 9%; and when DA exceeded 50%, rates were designated to reach 30%, 20%, and 10%. With the 8th Pay Commission reset, rates normalize back to primary baseline tiers, supported by strong minimum floor protections.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Minimum Guaranteed HRA Floors: Shielding Lower-Level Staff</h2>
        <p>
          A critical reform instituted by Central Pay Commissions is the <strong>Statutory Minimum HRA Guarantee</strong>. Without a floor limit, an employee serving at Pay Level 1 in a metropolitan area like Mumbai or Delhi (where starting basic is ₹18,000 under 7th CPC or ~₹41,040 under 8th CPC) would receive an inadequate monthly rental allocation relative to market conditions.
        </p>
        <p>
          To resolve this, the government mandates that regardless of percentage calculations, HRA cannot drop below specified monthly minimum values:
        </p>
        <ul class="bullet-feature-list">
          <li><strong>Class X Minimum Floor:</strong> Established at ₹5,400 under the 7th CPC; expected to be revised upward to <strong>₹10,800 to ₹12,000 per month</strong> under the 8th CPC.</li>
          <li><strong>Class Y Minimum Floor:</strong> Established at ₹3,600 under the 7th CPC; expected to be revised upward to <strong>₹7,200 to ₹8,000 per month</strong> under the 8th CPC.</li>
          <li><strong>Class Z Minimum Floor:</strong> Established at ₹1,800 under the 7th CPC; expected to be revised upward to <strong>₹3,600 to ₹4,000 per month</strong> under the 8th CPC.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          This provision ensures that Multi-Tasking Staff (MTS), junior clerks, and constabulary personnel stationed in major urban hubs maintain basic living standards.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Transport Allowance (TA) and The Dual-Compounding Factor</h2>
        <p>
          The <strong>Transport Allowance (TA)</strong> compensates employees for daily commuting between their residence and official duty station. Unlike HRA, which does not attract DA, Transport Allowance features an unusual compounding structure:
        </p>
        <div class="callout-box">
          <h4>The TA Calculation Equation:</h4>
          <p>
            <strong>Total Monthly Commute Allowance</strong> = <em>Base Transport Allowance (as per Pay Level and City) + [Base TA × Applicable DA %]</em>
          </p>
          <p>
            For officers and executives serving in Higher Transport Allowance Cities (Class X/Y urban hubs), the base TA scales higher (e.g., ₹7,200 or ₹3,600) compared to non-urban postings. When the 8th Pay Commission basic scales advance, the Base TA is similarly adjusted, providing an immediate lift to monthly commuting allocations.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

